1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,993
Here is the current magnetron as one complete C A D assembly.

2
00:00:03,993 --> 00:00:14,757
This walkthrough combines the original Advanced Tinkering head with our single coax power connection, one inch service tube and twelve one piece P T F E screw insulators with outward lips.

3
00:00:14,757 --> 00:00:18,765
The assembly contains one hundred and twenty nine component instances.

4
00:00:18,765 --> 00:00:24,548
We will separate its functional layers, inspect what each part does, and bring the gun back together.

5
00:00:24,548 --> 00:00:29,300
Proposed changes and reference purchased parts remain identified as study geometry.

6
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,006
This exploded view shows the main layers along their shared axis.

7
00:00:35,006 --> 00:00:39,694
At the target end are the dark space shield, target clamp and two inch target.

8
00:00:39,694 --> 00:00:43,841
The copper cooling body and copper backplate form the cooled cathode.

9
00:00:43,841 --> 00:00:47,507
The magnets sit in dry wells and touch the steel return yoke.

10
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:49,370
The spacer surrounds that pack.

11
00:00:49,370 --> 00:00:54,298
Farther back, the main P T F E ring separates the live spacer from grounded Boden.

12
00:00:54,298 --> 00:00:59,767
The long services and most small fasteners are temporarily hidden so the stack is readable.

13
00:01:01,567 --> 00:01:06,332
The two copper parts are now separated and the backplate is turned for inspection.

14
00:01:06,332 --> 00:01:07,669
This is a viewing pose.

15
00:01:07,669 --> 00:01:13,830
The cooling body provides the target contact face, the annular cooling channel and the blind magnet wells.

16
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:17,491
The backplate closes the channel and carries the coolant ports.

17
00:01:17,491 --> 00:01:19,119
The magnet wells remain dry.

18
00:01:19,119 --> 00:01:24,466
The twelve rear screws join these copper parts independently of the target retaining screws.

19
00:01:24,466 --> 00:01:28,767
That separation allows target changes without opening the water enclosure.

20
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:33,300
Actual target cooling still depends on the quality of its contact with copper.

21
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:38,954
Two O rings separate the water channel from the dry magnet spaces.

22
00:01:38,954 --> 00:01:44,677
Their nominal sizes are fourteen point five by two millimetres and thirty five by two millimetres.

23
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:49,349
The backplate closes onto the cooling body and the rear screws retain the joint.

24
00:01:49,349 --> 00:01:55,189
The revealed annulus has opposed inlet and outlet ports, so water can take both semicircular routes.

25
00:01:55,189 --> 00:01:59,335
Moving markers illustrate those routes; they are not a flow simulation.

26
00:01:59,335 --> 00:02:05,700
The displayed installed O ring shapes are occupied volume representations, not calculated rubber deformation.

27
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,843
The magnet pack approaches from behind.

28
00:02:09,843 --> 00:02:13,267
Twelve outer rods sit on a fifty millimetre pitch circle.

29
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:15,731
Two stacked discs form the center magnet.

30
00:02:15,731 --> 00:02:20,717
The center target facing pole is north and the outer target facing poles are south.

31
00:02:20,717 --> 00:02:25,643
The mild steel yoke supports the rear faces and provides the magnetic return path.

32
00:02:25,643 --> 00:02:27,686
A separate screw retains the yoke.

33
00:02:27,686 --> 00:02:31,531
The creator describes this as a Type two unbalanced arrangement.

34
00:02:31,531 --> 00:02:38,500
We retain it as the baseline; this C A D view does not calculate field strength, magnetic balance or a power rating.

35
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,910
The stainless spacer surrounds the backplate and magnet yoke.

36
00:02:43,910 --> 00:02:48,349
A seventy six by two millimetre O ring seals its joint to the cooling body.

37
00:02:48,349 --> 00:02:51,368
Twelve long screws secure this live upper assembly.

38
00:02:51,368 --> 00:02:54,978
The main P T F E ring then sits between the spacer and Boden.

39
00:02:54,978 --> 00:02:59,772
A sixty two by two millimetre seal is used at each side of that insulating joint.

40
00:02:59,772 --> 00:03:03,560
These seals close the dry internal space against chamber vacuum.

41
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:09,833
The main P T F E ring has both locating features and separate clearances for the different screw families.

42
00:03:11,633 --> 00:03:15,899
Here is the updated bottom body with twelve one piece P T F E insulators.

43
00:03:15,899 --> 00:03:22,912
Each insulator combines the shank sleeve, the shoulder beneath the metal screw head, the guard wall and the outward lip.

44
00:03:22,912 --> 00:03:28,405
The shoulder is captured by the screw, so the part does not depend on a separate guard fixing.

45
00:03:28,405 --> 00:03:32,729
The lip emerges from the hole and extends sideways over the grounded face.

46
00:03:32,729 --> 00:03:36,294
The proposed head pockets are enlarged to twelve millimetres.

47
00:03:36,294 --> 00:03:40,443
These base screws engage the live spacer, so their heads are also live.

48
00:03:40,443 --> 00:03:44,300
Their guards remain an unqualified electrical insulation proposal.

49
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:49,634
One continuous coax runs through the atmospheric service tube.

50
00:03:49,634 --> 00:03:54,706
A short exposed shield band contacts the clamp on a conductive carrier anchored to Boden.

51
00:03:54,706 --> 00:03:58,753
The protected center continues separately to the live copper backplate.

52
00:03:58,753 --> 00:04:05,194
A copper stand off passes through the existing yoke clearance, bringing the larger ring terminal behind the yoke.

53
00:04:05,194 --> 00:04:07,588
A longer M four terminal screw retains it.

54
00:04:07,588 --> 00:04:10,552
The two blue coolant hoses remain beside this route.

55
00:04:10,552 --> 00:04:14,769
Clamp and terminal shapes use catalogue dimensions as reference envelopes.

56
00:04:14,769 --> 00:04:19,500
The cable and complete connection still need electrical selection and verification.

57
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:27,577
Below Boden, a centering ring, seal, tube flange and two clamp halves form the K F sixteen style connection.

58
00:04:27,577 --> 00:04:29,205
Six screws retain the clamp.

59
00:04:29,205 --> 00:04:40,016
The revised tube preserves the smaller neck at this interface, then expands to a twenty five point four millimetre outside diameter for the selected one inch chamber compression adapter.

60
00:04:40,016 --> 00:04:43,619
The two coolant hoses and coax share its atmospheric interior.

61
00:04:43,619 --> 00:04:46,525
The purchased chamber adapter is not modeled here.

62
00:04:46,525 --> 00:04:53,500
Tube length, coolant fittings and the compatible K F seal remain reference details rather than confirmed supplier C A D.

63
00:04:55,300 --> 00:05:00,668
At the front, the two inch target seats on copper and the retaining ring overlaps its edge.

64
00:05:00,668 --> 00:05:03,794
Twelve separate front screws apply the clamping load.

65
00:05:03,794 --> 00:05:08,395
The displayed two millimetre target thickness is a reconstruction placeholder.

66
00:05:08,395 --> 00:05:13,881
Next, the grounded outer tube surrounds the head, and three radial screws secure it to Boden.

67
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:19,367
The dark space shield locates at the target end and is retained by six smaller radial screws.

68
00:05:19,367 --> 00:05:23,378
Its opening exposes the target while screening surrounding hardware.

69
00:05:23,378 --> 00:05:26,209
Neither outer shield is the primary vacuum seal.

70
00:05:26,209 --> 00:05:30,633
These exploded motions are explanatory, not a qualified workshop procedure.

71
00:05:32,433 --> 00:05:35,515
The final cutaway brings the whole design together.

72
00:05:35,515 --> 00:05:38,777
Water occupies the copper annulus and connected hoses.

73
00:05:38,777 --> 00:05:43,308
The dry interior and central service tube communicate with room atmosphere.

74
00:05:43,308 --> 00:05:48,262
Chamber vacuum is outside the sealed stack, including beneath the grounded shield.

75
00:05:48,262 --> 00:05:50,981
The face seals define that pressure boundary.

76
00:05:50,981 --> 00:05:56,600
The copper head, spacer and target are electrically live; Boden and the shields are grounded.

77
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:00,467
Our latest insulators and coax are integrated mechanically here.

78
00:06:00,467 --> 00:06:06,327
Magnetic performance, cooling capacity, RF ratings and arc resistance still require verification.

79
00:06:06,327 --> 00:06:09,167
The view then returns to the complete exterior.
